{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>You shall not add</b> — for example, five passages in tefillin, five species in the lulav, five tzitzit. And likewise \"you shall not subtract,\" in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 4:2). And thus they said in the Sifrei: From where do we learn that one may not add to the lulav or to the tzitzit? Scripture says: \"you shall not add.\" And from where do we learn that one may not subtract from them? Scripture says: \"you shall not subtract.\" From where do we learn that if one began reciting the Priestly Blessing, he may not say, \"Since I have begun to bless, I will say, 'May YHWH, the Elohim of your fathers, add to you'\"? Scripture says: <b>the matter</b> — even a single matter you may not add to it.\n\nBut they did not say this only regarding these cases, for even one who dwells in the sukkah on the eighth day with intention transgresses by lashes, as mentioned in tractate Rosh HaShanah (Rosh HaShanah 28). And likewise if one makes the festival six days, he transgresses this prohibition (לאו). And in my opinion, even if one invents a festival to perform a commandment on its own, such as if he made a festival in a month he invented of his own accord like Jeroboam (1 Kings 12:33), he transgresses the prohibition.\n\nAnd thus they said (Megillah 14) regarding the matter of reading the Megillah: one hundred and eighty prophets arose for Israel, and they did not subtract from or add to what is written in the Torah even a single letter — except for the reading of the Megillah. What is the derivation? etc. And in the Jerusalem Talmud (Megillah 1:7): eighty-five elders, and among them several prophets, were distressed over the matter. They said: It is written, \"These are the commandments that YHWH commanded Moses\" — these are the commandments that were commanded through Moses; thus Moses said to us, and no other prophet is destined to innovate a matter for you, yet Mordecai and Esther wish to innovate a matter for us. They did not move from there, deliberating the matter, until HaKadosh Baruch Hu illuminated their eyes, etc. See — this commandment was forbidden to them; if so, it falls under \"you shall not add to it.\"\n\nRather, we did not learn [the prohibition] against adding according to a prophet except from the verse that says <b>these are the commandments</b> — no prophet is permitted to innovate anything in it from now on. And what the Sages enacted as a safeguard (גדר), such as secondary categories of forbidden relations (שניות לעריות) and similar things, that itself is a commandment from the Torah, provided one knows that they are on account of this safeguard and are not from HaKadosh Baruch Hu in the Torah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The phrase ה' אלהי אבותיכם יוסף עליכם alludes to Numbers 6:27 paired with Deuteronomy 1:11; rendered as it appears in the Talmudic discussion at Rosh HaShanah 28a-b. הקב\"ה expanded as HaKadosh Baruch Hu throughout, consistent with the register for reverent abbreviations."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:44:58.741668539Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}